<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!-- Part 14 e2e scenario: the Optional counterpart to
     test_vt_objecttype_contradiction.NodeSet2, structurally identical
     (same MotorAType/MotorBType sibling ObjectTypes, same BaseType/SubType
     override of "Drive"), except the ModellingRule is Optional instead of
     Mandatory. Deliberately expected to find NO contradiction: this locks
     in a real, understood limitation of the encoding, not a bug.

     _add_all_values_from asserts allValuesFrom unconditionally for both
     Mandatory and Optional (see its own comment), but the existence-forcing
     restriction (_add_cardinality's minQualifiedCardinality) is only added
     for Mandatory/MandatoryPlaceholder. Without it, BaseType/SubType only
     carry:
       BaseType rdfs:subClassOf [ onProperty hasDrive; allValuesFrom MotorAType ]
       SubType  rdfs:subClassOf BaseType,
                    [ onProperty hasDrive; allValuesFrom MotorBType ]
     allValuesFrom is vacuously satisfied by an instance with zero hasDrive
     edges, so hasDrive being Functional and MotorAType/MotorBType being
     disjoint (both true here, exactly as in the Mandatory scenario) still
     can't force a contradiction: "the component may simply be absent" is
     the correct reading of Optional, and remains a valid model of SubType.
     This is inherent to what Optional means in OPC UA, not something a
     smarter axiom could fix without wrongly forcing every optional slot to
     be filled (which is exactly why _add_all_values_from's own comment
     forbids adding someValuesFrom there). Confirmed with the real HermiT
     reasoner (check_consistency.py's expect-none mode): the ontology stays
     consistent. -->
